They have to have a security officer who's cleared.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:11.180
They have to have a vaulted space where you can have a classified conversation.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:12.180
Okay.

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:20.940
There are different provisos that have to be met before you can actually be granted or

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.420
accept that contract.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:24.420
Okay.

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:25.420
Yeah.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:32.060
And that sort of leads into my next question on, and you might not be able to answer this,

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:36.940
of the mechanics of the way this works is, all right, I'm trying to visualize working

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:45.020
at the CIA without knowing, do you picture in a huge building, you know, lots of hundreds

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:55.820
of people working in, maybe thousands, is it, so do you know, as a person, an analyst

00:18:55.820 --> 00:19:01.860
or an operations person working at the CIA full time, do you know, or is there anyone

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:04.820
that knows everything that's going on?

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:05.820
Very few people.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:08.180
You can comment on three hands.

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:09.180
Wow.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:17.300
So, you know, the idea of the need to know is very, very important.

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:20.180
I don't care how high your clearances are.

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:25.460
If you don't have a specific need to know about an operation, you're not going to know.

00:19:25.460 --> 00:19:26.460
Okay.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:31.100
It wasn't until the end of my career, when I was the deputy director's executive

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:36.620
assistant that I finally had access to everything that the CIA was doing.

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:38.660
And I'll tell you something crazy.

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:41.660
There was one specific compartment.

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:49.500
I was read into six or eight compartments above top secret that were, that were very

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:55.420
closely held, extraordinarily sensitive operations around the world.

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:59.260
And there was a development one day in one of these compartments.

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:00.020
I was briefing.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:08.360
the deputy director for operations and all of the associate deputy directors, operations,

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:14.680
intelligence, science and technology, administration, counterintelligence, counterterrorism, right?

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:17.780
And the staff.

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:28.920
And as soon as I started the briefing, I said, there's a fascinating cable in from

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:30.480
the place.

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:33.920
And those were the only words I got out of my mouth.

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:38.520
And the ADDO, the associate deputy director for operations, stopped me and said, wait a

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:42.960
minute, not everybody in the room is cleared for that.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:45.400
The deputy directors of the CIA.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:46.520
Yeah.

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:50.680
And they weren't cleared for the information.

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:56.960
So, and I actually remember because I was listening to you telling the story

00:20:56.960 --> 00:21:00.760
in detail of capturing Abu Zabeda.

00:21:00.760 --> 00:21:07.560
And you, it did surprise me towards the end of that story when he was being taken out by

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:09.400
the rendition team.

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:14.720
You mentioned that, that you weren't even as the person who caught him, you weren't even

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:16.960
allowed to know where he was going.

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:19.040
No, I need to know.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:21.880
My job was to catch him and turn him over.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:22.880
And that was it.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:23.880
Yeah.

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:24.880
Wow.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:25.880
And they stuck to that.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.840
And I was like the guy on the rendition team who stopped me that night.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:29.840
Yeah.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:30.840
He said, come on.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:31.840
And I said, who are you?

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:32.840
He had a black mask on.

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:33.840
I said, who are you?

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:38.600
He lifted up his mask and he was my last boss.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:42.280
And I said, where are you taking him?

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:44.600
And he said, oh dude, I'm sorry, you don't have a need to know.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:45.960
And I said, yeah, that's cool.

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.160
And he said, well, who is the guy?

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:50.640
And I said, oh man, I'm sorry, you don't have a need to know.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:51.640
Wow.

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:52.640
My job was to catch him.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:53.640
Yeah.

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:54.640
Turn him over.

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:59.720
My job was to take him from point A to point B. It didn't matter what his name was.

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:00.720
Wow.

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:01.880
Yeah.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:07.080
So what, well, that seems to be, it would be need to know who you're transporting to

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:08.080
me.

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:13.840
But like, so who determines what that, or is it just like if you were to tell him,

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:18.800
then you might be subject to some sort of internal review that determined you did

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:19.800
the wrong thing.

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:20.800
Yeah.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:21.800
There would be an internal sanction.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:24.400
I would be investigated by the Office of Security.

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:27.360
I'd probably be called in before a performance board.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:28.360
Okay.

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:33.920
You know, you might be denied promotion for a year or two years for a violation like

00:22:33.920 --> 00:22:34.920
that.

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:42.320
And what is, and again, stupid question, but when people use the term, you're read

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:50.000
into a program, is that literally just somebody signing off on something or is it a much

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:51.000
bigger?

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:52.000
Okay.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:53.000
Bigger than that.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:54.000
So I'll give you an example.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:23:02.440
On my first day as the ADDO's executive assistant, I walked into the office that

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:06.560
morning and I said, so what are we doing?

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:08.000
And he says, you know what?

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:09.000
I can't tell you.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:13.880
You have to go up to the sixth floor and I knew what office he meant.

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:16.560
You have to go up to the sixth floor and you have to be read in first and then

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:18.840
come back and we'll talk about it.

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:23.640
So I go up to the sixth floor and they had six separate secrecy agreements

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:25.520
laid out on a desk.

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:30.680
And I had to go through each secrecy agreement and promise to take everything I learned

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:35.720
to the grave, never talk about it unless it's specifically declassified.

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:38.160
And most of it has been.

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:42.680
And then I signed six times each one of the secrecy agreements.

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:46.200
And I said, okay, so what's up?

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:49.360
And then he said, well, number one, we're going to invader rock and

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:50.640
overthrow us that I'm the same.

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:52.640
Number two, we're going to do this.

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:54.080
Number three, we're going to do that.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:55.080
Number four.

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:56.080
Yeah.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:57.080
Wow.

00:23:57.080 --> 00:24:00.680
So I went back to the office and I said to my boss, I go, we're going to

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:01.680
invade Iraq.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:07.560
And he said, I know it's crazy, but yeah, yeah, we're going to invade Iraq.

00:24:07.560 --> 00:24:08.560
Wow.

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:15.120
So is it difficult or do you just develop sort of a extra sense in your

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:16.680
brain and communicating?

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:25.200
I guess maybe you're even practice that as a CIA field operative that of what

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:27.600
you can say and what you can't.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:31.080
Does it sort of get to be second nature where you're not constantly

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:32.080
ice?

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:33.080
Okay.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:34.080
That's a good question.

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:37.000
It does, but it can take years to get to that point.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:38.000
Yeah.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:41.480
Over the course of those years where you're learning what you can say

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:44.920
and what you can't say even internally, you get slapped down a

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:45.920
couple of times.

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:48.600
And you kind of learn the hard way.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:49.760
I was giving a briefing once.

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:53.920
I'd been on the job, I'm going to say three years.

00:24:53.920 --> 00:25:00.960
And I was giving a briefing and I mentioned a location.

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:03.880
Now everybody in the briefing is cleared, right?

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:07.600
It's an internal briefing, but I mentioned a location.

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:10.520
And during the break, one of the senior officers came up to me and she

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:13.520
said, you should never have mentioned that location.

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:15.720
They didn't have a need to know the location.

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:18.840
And she said, it's a security violation.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.440
And I said, my God, I'm sorry.

00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:23.560
It never even occurred to me that the location would be.

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:25.120
I mean, everybody's cleared.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:28.640
And she said, I know that you just got here.

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:31.640
I was in my three-year probationary period.

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:32.880
She said, I know you just got here.

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:34.440
I know you didn't do it on purpose.

00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:36.640
So I won't report you to security.

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:40.640
But I mean, here we are 30 years later.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:41.640
Yeah.

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:43.840
I still remember all the details of that slapdown.

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:44.840
Yeah.

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:50.800
Yeah, that seems like at first it would almost be overwhelming of like you

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:53.960
would be too paranoid that you're going to say the wrong thing.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:54.960
Oh, yeah.

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:55.960
Yeah.

00:25:55.960 --> 00:25:56.960
Yeah.

00:25:56.960 --> 00:26:01.520
Because, you know, your polygraph, before you get hired, after three years

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:03.840
and then every five years for the rest of your career.

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:06.360
And so it's going to come up in the polygraph.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:07.360
Yeah.

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:08.360
You've forgotten about it.

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.160
They're going to ask you an open question.

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:15.880
Have you ever exposed classified information to any person not entitled to

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:16.880
receive it?

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:19.240
And you have to go back and like, yeah, you know what?

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:24.160
In a briefing with the Israelis, I may have said this or I accidentally

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:31.600
mentioned to the Brits when I was in a liaison meeting, you know, all this stuff floods back.

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:35.680
And so you have to try to get through it.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:40.440
Was that a big change going from an analyst?

00:26:40.440 --> 00:26:46.400
Obviously the jobs you're tasked with are entirely different.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:52.800
But from analysts to operations, was that like a completely different

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:56.920
skillset training that you had to go in through forever?

00:26:56.920 --> 00:26:57.920
No comparison.

00:26:57.920 --> 00:26:58.920
Okay.

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:04.360
As an analyst, you know, in the beginning, I only thought about being an analyst.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:07.840
I kind of considered myself a kind of an intellectual.

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:11.200
While I was working on my PhD in international affairs, I had a bachelor's

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:16.080
degree in Middle Eastern studies and a master's in policy analysis.

00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:20.880
An analyst sort of sit in a cubicle every day and think the big thoughts and

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:25.840
write the president's daily brief and write policy papers for the president and

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:29.600
the vice president, the secretaries of state and defense, the national

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:32.320
security advisor.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:35.680
And you know, you're the substantive expert on whatever your issue

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:36.680
happens to be.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:43.160
My time was Iraq before, during and after the first Gulf War.

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:45.320
And you know, it's great.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:48.680
Sometimes you find yourself at a position like August 2, 1990, one of the most

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:50.880
important days of my life.

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:52.200
Iraq invades Kuwait.

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:54.400
I get into the office around 7 a.m.

00:27:54.400 --> 00:27:57.720
As soon as I walk in, my boss says, don't take your jacket off.

00:27:57.720 --> 00:27:59.040
We're going to the White House.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:04.680
Now I was 25 years old.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:05.680
We get in a car.

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:06.680
We go to the White House.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:10.160
They usher us in to the friggin oval office.

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:14.880
The president, the vice president, the national security advisor, the director

00:28:14.880 --> 00:28:18.280
of the CIA, my boss and me.

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:19.280
And we sit down.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:20.280
I'm a nervous wreck.

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:26.280
And I remember looking around and thinking, my friends would not believe

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:27.280
me.

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:31.000
I told them what I was doing right now.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:35.000
So the president says, this was George H. W. Bush.

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:38.360
He says, well, now what do we do?

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:41.560
And everybody turns and looks at me.

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:42.560
And I was at home.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:43.560
Yeah.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:44.560
It was that.

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:45.560
It was that crazy.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:46.560
Yeah.

00:28:46.560 --> 00:28:47.560
It was that crazy.

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:49.840
Dick Cheney was the one that sort of led the meeting, which was also

00:28:49.840 --> 00:28:50.920
crazy.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:55.000
He was the one who at the time Cheney was, what was Cheney?

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.360
He was Secretary of Defense.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:28:58.360
Defense.

00:28:58.360 --> 00:28:59.360
Yeah.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:00.360
Secretary of Defense.

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:02.760
He's the one that sort of led the questioning.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:10.920
And it was just, it was one of the most bizarre, like defining events of my

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:11.920
career.

00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:15.480
Now, that's a once in a career kind of thing.

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:16.480
Okay.

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:22.800
On the other hand, when you go into operations, it's like, it's an

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:25.120
entirely different world.

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:26.120
Yeah.

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:30.280
Whether it's, you know, recruiting spies to steal secrets, which is kind of

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:38.880
the basis of operations or running covert operations or actions or planting bugs or

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:43.880
videos or liaising with foreign intelligence services or counterterrorism

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:47.840
operations or the most sensitive operations of them all, which are

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:50.560
counterintelligence operations.

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:55.920
And then within each of those categories, everything is compartmentalized.

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:59.960
And you might not be able to answer this is.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:06.500
are there different specialists in each of those categories

00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:11.600
like within operations or do you do all of that as an operation?

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:12.200
Oh, no, no.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:14.500
There are definitely specialists.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:15.000
OK.

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:21.800
The guy, for example, he was the unlikeliest operations officer.

00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:29.400
He was kind of a nerd and an intellectual and he had gone to MIT.

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:31.900
And I'm like, what are you doing here?

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:34.800
Like, why aren't you a professor somewhere or something?

00:30:34.800 --> 00:30:37.200
I don't think he was a nuclear scientist.

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:42.600
You know, when you're trying to infiltrate, let's say, the North Korean nuclear program,

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:45.200
you need people who can talk the talk.

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:46.800
I don't know what to say.

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:48.000
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:50.600
I'll give you an example.

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:53.900
In training, we were doing a training exercise.

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:59.700
And these training exercises were absolutely real life, right?

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:03.400
In fact, I remember going into the field for the first time and being shocked

00:31:03.400 --> 00:31:07.900
at how real life was exactly like the training.

00:31:07.900 --> 00:31:09.600
It was remarkable.

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:19.600
So I'm in this training exercise and I'm supposed to be at a diplomatic cocktail party overseas.

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.600
So, you know, we're wearing suits and we're at a restaurant.

00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:27.100
We had rented this private, you know, back room and everybody's got a cocktail

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:29.700
and waiters are walking around with hors d'oeuvres and stuff.

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:31.900
It's the real deal.

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:33.600
And I walk up to this woman.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:34.900
I say, hello, how are you?

00:31:34.900 --> 00:31:35.900
Fine, how are you?

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:40.300
I said, I'm, you know, John Kiriakou from the American Embassy.

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:44.700
I think that we called our country the Republic of Victoria or some.

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:48.300
We made up some, some name of some country.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:49.800
So I said, how about you?

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:50.600
What do you do for a living?

00:31:50.600 --> 00:31:51.700
Oh, I work at the port.

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:52.700
I said, oh, that's nice.

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:55.500
I'm thinking, what do I care about this woman?

00:31:55.500 --> 00:31:58.600
She's, I'm not interesting to me.

00:31:58.600 --> 00:31:59.700
Oh, what do you do at the port?

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:02.500
Oh, I process this paperwork.

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:03.100
Oh, okay.

00:32:03.100 --> 00:32:03.900
That's interesting.

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:06.100
It's actually not, but I'm trying to be nice.

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:06.500
Yeah.

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:11.700
And then she says something about, oh, I, I almost missed the party

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:13.300
because I had to work late.

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:19.800
There's a shipment of trichloromethyl ethylene that, that, you know,

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:22.100
didn't have the proper paperwork.

00:32:22.100 --> 00:32:22.400
Okay.

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:23.400
Well, what the fuck do I know?

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:24.600
I don't know what that is.

00:32:24.600 --> 00:32:25.400
Yeah.

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:26.800
I was like, oh, I'm sorry to hear that.

